epitaphDeed
  who counted every star and gave each one a name
  who was beloved of no one, and mourned by all
  who kept the lamps burning through the longest night
  who told a truth that no one wished to hear
  who built the western wall with their own two hands
  who fed the hungry years, and was forgotten by the fed
  who waited at the gate for a ship that never came
  who feared nothing but the sound of their own name
  who sang the dead to sleep for forty winters
  who bargained with the river, and lost
  who held a city on their shoulders and called it light
  who loved a ghost, and was faithful to the end
  who wrote the names of the lost until their hand gave out
  who stood last upon the bridge as the waters rose
  who forgave the ones who buried them too soon
  who carried the dead across the black water for no coin
  who laughed once, and the whole of the ward remembers it
  who was small, and kind, and is missed
  who dug the deepest grave and lay down in it first
  who kept a promise made before the world was drowned

epitaphCloser
  Rest now, and do not dream.
  The river remembers your name.
  Sleep beneath the patient stone.
  What you carried is set down.
  The bell has rung for you. Be still.
  Go gently into the quiet.
  The lamps are lit. You are not alone.
  Nothing is asked of you now.
  Let the long dusk take you kindly.
  You are numbered among the remembered.

